  <div id="lessonContent">
    <h2>1. What is an Event?</h2>
    <p><b>Event = Something That Happens on the Page.</b> User clicks a button, moves the mouse, types in a box, presses a key, loads the page - All of these are events.</p>
    <p>JavaScript waits for an event, and when the event happens, it runs your function. This is called <b>Event Handling</b>.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>Real Life Example:</b> A doorbell. You do nothing until someone presses the bell. When the bell rings (event), you go to the door (function). JavaScript works exactly the same way.</div>
    <h2>2. LIVE DEMO - Events Playground</h2>
    <p>Try everything - Click, Hover, Type, Press Key. Watch the log:</p>
    <div class="demo-card">
      <h3 id="eventTitle" style="margin:0">Try Events Below 👇</h3>
      <div class="hover-box" id="hoverBox" onmouseover="logEvent('Mouse Over - Mouse entered box')" onmouseout="logEvent('Mouse Out - Mouse left box')" onclick="logEvent('Click - You clicked the box')">🖱️ Hover Me / Click Me - Mouse Event Box</div>
      <input class="demo-input" id="typeInput" type="text" placeholder="Type here - oninput event will fire" oninput="logEvent('Typing: ' + this.value)" onfocus="logEvent('Focus - You clicked inside input')" onblur="logEvent('Blur - You left the input')">
      <input class="demo-input" id="keyInput" type="text" placeholder="Press any key here - onkeydown event" onkeydown="logEvent('Key Pressed: ' + event.key)">
      <button class="btn btn-js" onclick="logEvent('Button Clicked - onclick event')">Click Me 👆</button>
      <button class="btn" onclick="clearLog()">Clear Log 🧹</button>
      <div class="log-box" id="eventLog">Event Log will show here...<br></div>
      <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:13px;color:#64748b">Event Log = Proof that events are working.</p>
    </div>
    <h2>3. All Events - Full Table</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Category</th><th>Event</th><th>When It Happens</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Mouse</b></td><td>onclick</td><td>When user clicks</td><td>Button click, Like button</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Mouse</b></td><td>onmouseover</td><td>Mouse enters element</td><td>Show tooltip, Change color</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Mouse</b></td><td>onmouseout</td><td>Mouse leaves element</td><td>Hide tooltip, Back to normal</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Mouse</b></td><td>ondblclick</td><td>Double click</td><td>Open file, Edit text</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Keyboard</b></td><td>onkeydown</td><td>Key is pressed down</td><td>Game controls, Search on Enter</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Keyboard</b></td><td>onkeyup</td><td>Key is released</td><td>Show typed text</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Form / Input</b></td><td>oninput</td><td>Every time user types</td><td>Live preview, Character count</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Form / Input</b></td><td>onchange</td><td>Value changes + input loses focus</td><td>Dropdown change, Checkbox</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Form / Input</b></td><td>onfocus</td><td>Input gets focus</td><td>Highlight input box</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Form / Input</b></td><td>onblur</td><td>Input loses focus</td><td>Check input when user leaves</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Form</b></td><td>onsubmit</td><td>Form is submitted</td><td>Check form before sending</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Page</b></td><td>onload</td><td>Page finishes loading</td><td>Show welcome message</td></tr>
    </table>
    <h2>4. Way 1 - Inline Event (In HTML)</h2>
    <p>This is the easy way you already know. Write the event directly in the HTML tag.</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;button</span> <span class="attr">onclick</span>=<span class="val">"sayHello()"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Click Me</span><span class="tag">&lt;/button&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;input</span> <span class="attr">oninput</span>=<span class="val">"showText()"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><br><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
<span class="text">function sayHello() { alert("Hello!"); }</span><br>
<span class="text">function showText() { console.log("Typing..."); }</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>5. Way 2 - addEventListener (Best Way)</h2>
    <p>This is the professional way. Keep HTML clean, write all events in JavaScript.</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;button</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"myBtn"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Click Me</span><span class="tag">&lt;/button&gt;</span><br><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
<span class="text">let btn = document.getElementById("myBtn");</span><br><br>
<span class="text">btn.addEventListener("click", function() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">alert("Button Clicked!");</span><br>
<span class="text">});</span><br><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Same with named function</span><br>
<span class="text">btn.addEventListener("mouseover", changeColor);</span><br>
<span class="text">function changeColor() { btn.style.backgroundColor = "red"; }</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <div class="note"><b>Why addEventListener is Better?</b> 1. One element can have many events - Click + Mouseover together. 2. HTML stays clean. 3. You can remove the event later. In real jobs, everyone uses addEventListener.</div>
    <h2>6. Event Object - What Happened?</h2>
    <p>When an event happens, JavaScript sends an <b>event object</b> with details - Which key was pressed? Where was the mouse? Which element was clicked?</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Which key was pressed?</span><br>
<span class="text">document.addEventListener("keydown", function(event) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">console.log("Key: " + event.key); // "a", "Enter", "Escape"</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">if (event.key === "Enter") { console.log("You pressed Enter!"); }</span><br>
<span class="text">});</span><br><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Which element was clicked?</span><br>
<span class="text">document.addEventListener("click", function(event) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">console.log("Clicked: " + event.target.id);</span><br>
<span class="text">});</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>7. Complete Example - Live Character Counter</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;body&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;textarea</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"msg"</span> <span class="attr">placeholder</span>=<span class="val">"Type your message"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="tag">&lt;/textarea&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;p</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"count"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">0 / 100 characters</span><span class="tag">&lt;/p&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let box = document.getElementById("msg");</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let countText = document.getElementById("count");</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">box.addEventListener("input", function() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let len = box.value.length;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">countText.textContent = len + " / 100 characters";</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">if (len > 100) { countText.style.color = "red"; }</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">else { countText.style.color = "black"; }</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">});</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/body&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/html&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>8. Common Mistakes</h2>
    <p>1. <b>Forgetting Brackets in Inline:</b> <code>onclick="sayHello"</code> ❌ - Will not run. Correct is <code>onclick="sayHello()"</code> ✅<br>2. <b>Brackets in addEventListener:</b> <code>btn.addEventListener("click", sayHello())</code> ❌ - Runs at once. Correct is <code>btn.addEventListener("click", sayHello)</code> ✅ - No brackets, pass the function.<br>3. <b>on vs No on:</b> In HTML use <code>onclick</code>. In addEventListener use <code>"click"</code> - No "on".</p>
    <h2>9. Homework</h2>
    <p>In Playground, build this:</p>
    <p>1. A box - on mouseover it turns red, on mouseout it turns back to blue<br>2. An input - on input, show live preview of what user types below the input<br>3. A password input - on keydown, if user presses Enter, show alert "Enter Pressed!"</p>
    <div class="box"><b>Remember:</b> Event = Something happens on page | onclick = Click | onmouseover = Mouse enters | onmouseout = Mouse leaves | onkeydown = Key pressed | oninput = Every time user types | onchange = Value changed | onfocus = Input gets focus | onblur = Input loses focus | addEventListener("click", function) = Best way, no "on", no brackets | event.key = Which key | event.target = Which element</div>
    <p><b>Next Step:</b> <a href="/lesson32">Lesson 32 - Form Validation</a> - Learn how to check forms before sending. Name empty? Password too short? Email valid?</p>
